    "icon": "data:image/png;base64,",
    "description": "Estimates survival (or retention) curves from time-to-event data with censoring, by the Kaplan-Meier or Fleming-Harrington method, overall or per group, with 95% confidence limits and the median time per group.",
    "category": "Analytics",
    "subcategory": "Statistics",
    "tags": [
        "survival analysis",
        "kaplan-meier",
        "fleming-harrington",
        "censoring",
        "retention",
        "churn",
        "time to event"
    ],
    "introductoryText": "Estimates the share of subjects (customers, machines, patients) still \"surviving\" (subscribed, working, alive) over time, from one record per subject: how long it was followed (**Time**) and whether the event happened at the end of that time (**Event**). Subjects still going at the end of their time are *censored*: they count while followed and are not treated as events.\n\n**How to set it up**: choose the Time field (a number of days, months...), the Event field (1 = event, 0 = censored; or 2 / 1; or true / false) and, optionally, a Group field to get one curve per group. Choose the method: Kaplan-Meier (the usual one) or Fleming-Harrington.\n\n**Outputs**: **Curve** has one record per group and time where something happened (plus time 0), with the survival estimate and its 95% confidence limits: draw it as a step line. **Summary** has one record per group with the counts and the median time.",
    "dependencies": "",
